# Captured from vanilla 26.2 (protocol 776) survival oracle.
# Packet: clientbound set_entity_data for a `/summon item` drop of
# {id:"minecraft:diamond_pickaxe",count:1,
# components:{"minecraft:repair_cost":7}}.
#
# ..        VarInt entity id (session-scoped; not compared)
# 08        metadata index 8 = ItemEntity.DATA_ITEM
# 07        serializer 7 = ITEM_STACK
# 01        VarInt stack count = 1
# c6 07     VarInt item registry id 966 = minecraft:diamond_pickaxe
# 01        DataComponentPatch: 1 added
# 00        DataComponentPatch: 0 removed
# 13        component type id 19 = minecraft:repair_cost
# 07        its payload: ByteBufCodecs.VAR_INT, so one byte
# ff        end-of-list sentinel
#
# repair_cost was UNMODELED when this was captured, and the three
# abandonment gates in tests/item_entity_metadata.rs used it as their
# unmodeled component. It is decoded now (a bare VarInt), so those
# gates rewrite this one type-id byte to a component that is still
# unmodeled — see `with_unmodeled_component`, which asserts both ids
# are one-byte VarInts so no offset here moves. The capture as-is is
# now a *complete*-decode gate instead, and it is the outside source
# for the width: consume no bytes and `07` reads as a metadata index,
# consume two and the `ff` terminator is eaten.
#
# The reason a component cannot simply be skipped, for the abandoning
# arm: a clientbound patch length-prefixes neither itself nor its
# components, so an unmodeled one ends the decode in place. Reading
# on would take `07` as a metadata index and `ff` as a truncated
# serializer VarInt — a plausible-looking misparse.
a1 e4 01 08 07 01 c6 07 01 00 13 07 ff
